From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ricktav <82380971+ricktav@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 15:42:13 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] dt-bindings: gpio: apple,d2365-pmic-gpio: Add the D2089

The Dialog D2089 PMIC of the A7 iPads (iPad Air, iPad mini 2) has the
D2207's PMU event block: iOS 12.5.8 AppleD2089PMU reads its 14 event
bytes at 0x50 and masks events at 0x70 + n / 8 (set = masked), as
AppleD2207PMU does. The Lightning charger needs it as an interrupt
controller for the PMU line and ADC completion.

Add its compatible, with the D2207's limits on apple,wake-events.

Assisted-by: LLM
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml  | 34 +++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml
index ed3f849fc..cfee44312 100644
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml
@@ -4,29 +4,30 @@
 $id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml#
 $schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
 
-title: Apple A8/A9/A10 PMIC GPIO and event interrupt controller
+title: Apple A7/A8/A9/A10 PMIC GPIO and event interrupt controller
 
 maintainers:
   - Paul Praschl <praschlpaul@g-p.at>
 
 description: |
-  The Dialog D2186 and D2207 PMICs used with the Apple A8 and A8X and the
-  D2333 and D2365 PMICs used in Apple A9 and A10 devices provide 21, 21, 21
-  and 24 GPIOs respectively. The controller is accessed through the regmap exported by the
-  parent Apple I2C PMIC device.
+  The Dialog D2089 PMIC used with the Apple A7, the D2186 and D2207 PMICs
+  used with the Apple A8 and A8X and the D2333 and D2365 PMICs used in Apple
+  A9 and A10 devices provide 21, 21, 21, 21 and 24 GPIOs respectively. The
+  controller is accessed through the regmap exported by the parent Apple I2C
+  PMIC device.
 
   The D2207 and D2365 also latch 112 and 240 PMU events respectively
   (charger, ADC, GPIO edges and others) in one read-to-clear block and
-  signal them on a single active-low line to the AP. As an interrupt
-  controller the node owns that block: the interrupt specifier is the PMU
-  event number and a flags cell. PMIC GPIO line N is event 0x18 + N on the
-  D2207 and 0x30 + N on the D2365, so GPIO consumers get their interrupt
-  through the GPIO specifier as well. Events are latched; what triggers a
-  GPIO event is the line configuration left by the boot firmware, so only
-  edge flags are accepted.
+  signal them on a single active-low line to the AP; the D2089 has the
+  D2207's event block. As an interrupt controller the node owns that block:
+  the interrupt specifier is the PMU event number and a flags cell. PMIC
+  GPIO line N is event 0x18 + N on the D2207 and 0x30 + N on the D2365, so
+  GPIO consumers get their interrupt through the GPIO specifier as well.
+  Events are latched; what triggers a GPIO event is the line configuration
+  left by the boot firmware, so only edge flags are accepted.
 
-  Interrupt 112 (D2207) or 240 (D2365), one past the last event, is the
-  event line itself. Its consumers run on every assertion of the line,
+  Interrupt 112 (D2089, D2207) or 240 (D2365), one past the last event, is
+  the event line itself. Its consumers run on every assertion of the line,
   before the event block is read, so that a consumer can act before the I2C
   read that tells which event it was (the Lightning charger withdraws its
   input-current grant there).
@@ -34,6 +35,7 @@ description: |
 properties:
   compatible:
     enum:
+      - apple,d2089-pmic-gpio
       - apple,d2186-pmic-gpio
       - apple,d2207-pmic-gpio
       - apple,d2333-pmic-gpio
@@ -97,7 +99,9 @@ allOf:
   - if:
       properties:
         compatible:
-          const: apple,d2207-pmic-gpio
+          enum:
+            - apple,d2089-pmic-gpio
+            - apple,d2207-pmic-gpio
     then:
       properties:
         apple,wake-events:
